{{- /*
  blog/load-post-from-bead — Step-1 boilerplate for blog prompts that operate
  on the post whose content lives in a bead. The blog suite stores the post
  BODY in the bead's description and the post TITLE in the bead's title; there
  is no post file on disk during the drafting phase. This fragment loads both
  into shell variables and writes the body to a temp file so downstream steps
  can `cat`/`grep` it.

  Callers resolve the target bead themselves (`.Session.BeadsIssue` when the
  conversation is linked to a blog bead, else `.Args.IssueID` when launched
  from the beads list) and pass it via a `dict`:

      Target: the resolved bead ID. Required.

  After this fragment runs, downstream steps can rely on:
      $post_title  — the bead title (the blog post title)
      $post_file   — path to a temp file holding the bead description (the
                     current post body, verbatim)

  Usage:

      {{ template "blog/shared/load-post-from-bead" (dict "Target" $target) }}
*/ -}}
{{- $id := .Target -}}
Load the post from the bead. The blog post body lives in the bead's
**description** and the post title in the bead's **title** — there is no draft
file on disk. Pull both, and write the body to a temp file so later steps can
read it:

```bash
bd show {{ $id }} --json > /tmp/bead-{{ $id }}.json
post_title=$(jq -r '.[0].title // ""' /tmp/bead-{{ $id }}.json)
post_file=$(mktemp "/tmp/blog-{{ $id }}-XXXXXX.md")
jq -r '.[0].description // ""' /tmp/bead-{{ $id }}.json > "$post_file"
if [[ ! -s "$post_file" ]]; then
  echo "ERROR: bead {{ $id }} has an empty description — no blog post body to work on." >&2
  echo "Seed it with the ideation prompt (or add content) before running this stage." >&2
  exit 1
fi
echo "post_title=$post_title"
echo "post_file=$post_file"
```

Downstream steps in the caller can then read `"$post_file"` to load the current
post body and use `$post_title` for the title.
